# modules/dast/active/checks.rules - the DAST active-tier script-check registry
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md tiers 3 and 4). It carries
# the §7.2 safe-active content-discovery checks (DAST-DISC-*) and the §7.3
# active-injection checks (DAST-INJ-*).
#
# This is the first checks.rules the DAST module ships: auth.sh and crawl.sh
# cover no check (they acquire a session and build the inventory), so this file
# registers the first DAST checks tension 12 computes coverage over and
# tension 15's filter chain filters. Every record here is a `script` check, not
# a pattern - it names the shell script that implements it and carries
# `coverage-scope: target`, DAST's cell (the config/scope.conf target id,
# §9.5.1). checks_registry_load globs every *.rules under modules/dast/, so a
# later probe's records may live in a sibling file or here; the linter enforces
# one owning module (DAST) for all of them. Only a file named `checks.rules`
# takes the script-check schema (lib/records.sh's path table), which is why the
# §7.2 and §7.3 checks share this one file rather than each getting its own.
#
# The TYPE TAG is a per-check floor that must match the phase table's own tier
# (modules/dast/engine.sh). The §7.2 discovery checks are tagged `safe-active`,
# matching `active/discovery.sh:safe`, so they run at `--intensity safe` and up;
# the §7.3 SQLi checks are tagged `active`, matching `active/sqli.sh:active`, so
# they need `--intensity active`. Neither family is tagged `intrusive` - the
# discovery probes are read-only GETs and the injection payloads are read-only
# and non-destructive (docs/DESIGN.md §7.2/§7.3), so the intensity opt-in is the
# gate, and requiring --allow-intrusive on top would be a second, undocumented
# one. (The tests/fixtures/ copy tags an example SQLi check `intrusive` only to
# exercise that filter arm; it is not this shipped catalog.)
#
# One check id per technique, because the DAST fingerprint (target, method,
# path_template, param_location, param_name) does not carry the technique: a
# single id would make an error-based and a time-based finding on the same
# parameter collide and dedupe to one. rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.1.1 NAME allows
# `_`, so the technique lives in NAME under the shared `INJ` family.

id: DAST-INJ-SQLI_ERROR-01
title: SQL injection (error-based) via request parameter
script: active/sqli.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-89
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Use parameterised queries (prepared statements) or an ORM that
  binds request-derived values, so a request parameter is never interpolated
  into SQL text. Return a generic error so a driver message never reaches the
  client, and run under a least-privilege database account.
references: CWE-89
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-SQLI_BOOLEAN-01
title: SQL injection (boolean-based) via request parameter
script: active/sqli.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-89
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Use parameterised queries (prepared statements) or an ORM that
  binds request-derived values, so a request parameter is never interpolated
  into SQL text. Add server-side input validation and run under a
  least-privilege database account.
references: CWE-89
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-SQLI_TIME-01
title: SQL injection (time-based blind) via request parameter
script: active/sqli.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-89
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Use parameterised queries (prepared statements) or an ORM that
  binds request-derived values, so a request parameter is never interpolated
  into SQL text. Add server-side input validation and run under a
  least-privilege database account.
references: CWE-89
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-LDAP_ERROR-01
title: LDAP injection (error-based) via request parameter
script: active/ldapi.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-90
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Escape LDAP filter metacharacters in request-derived values per
  RFC 4515 (the client library's own escaping helper or a parameterised filter
  builder), so a request parameter can never change the structure of an LDAP
  search filter or DN. Return a generic error so a client-library message never
  reaches the client, and bind the directory with a least-privilege account.
references: CWE-90
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-LDAP_BOOLEAN-01
title: LDAP injection (boolean-based) via request parameter
script: active/ldapi.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-90
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Escape LDAP filter metacharacters in request-derived values per
  RFC 4515 (the client library's own escaping helper or a parameterised filter
  builder), so a request parameter can never change the structure of an LDAP
  search filter. Add server-side input validation and bind the directory with a
  least-privilege account.
references: CWE-90
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-DISC-SENSITIVE-01
title: Sensitive file or version-control metadata is web-accessible
script: active/discovery.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-538
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Remove the file from the web root, or block it at the server/CDN
  (deny access to dotfiles, version-control directories, config and dump
  files). Rotate any credential that may have been exposed, and add a
  deployment check that fails when such a file is reachable.
references: CWE-538
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-DISC-BACKUP-01
title: Backup or temporary file is web-accessible
script: active/discovery.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-530
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Delete backup and editor temporary files from the web root and
  stop deployments from copying them there; block the known suffixes (.bak, ~,
  .old, .orig, .swp, ...) at the server or CDN, and rotate any secret the file
  may have disclosed.
references: CWE-530
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-DISC-CONTENT-01
title: Undisclosed resource discovered via content discovery
script: active/discovery.sh
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Confirm the resource is meant to be public; if not, remove it or
  move it behind authentication. Ensure every reachable endpoint enforces its
  own access control rather than relying on being unlinked.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-DISC-DIRLIST-01
title: Directory listing is enabled
script: active/discovery.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-548
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Disable automatic directory indexing (Apache `Options -Indexes`,
  nginx `autoindex off`, or the equivalent) and serve an explicit index or a
  403/404 so directory contents are never enumerated to clients.
references: CWE-548
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

# --- DAST-15: reflected XSS (docs/DESIGN.md §7.3, `active/xss.sh`) -----------
#
# Three ids for the same defect class for the SAME reason sqli has three above:
# the DAST location profile carries no component naming the CONTEXT a
# reflection landed in, so a single id would make an HTML-text and an
# attribute-value reflection on one parameter collide and dedupe to one. The
# context is what decides whether a given raw character is dangerous - a raw
# `"` is harmless in element text and a break-out inside a quoted attribute -
# so it is the thing a reader needs and the thing the remediation differs on.
#
# `tags: active`, matching the phase table's `active/xss.sh:active` floor. NOT
# `intrusive`: the probe sends one unique alphanumeric marker with `<`, `>`,
# `"` and `'` appended and nothing else - no script, no event handler, no
# `javascript:` URL, nothing that executes - so the value is inert wherever it
# lands and the active-intensity opt-in is the right and only gate.

id: DAST-INJ-XSS_REFLECTED_HTML-01
title: Reflected cross-site scripting - unescaped reflection in HTML text
script: active/xss.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: HTML-entity-encode request-derived data where it is written into
  element text, at the point of output rather than on input. Prefer a template
  engine that escapes by default and do not disable it for this value. Add a
  Content-Security-Policy without unsafe-inline as defence in depth.
references: CWE-79
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-XSS_REFLECTED_ATTR-01
title: Reflected cross-site scripting - unescaped reflection in an HTML attribute value
script: active/xss.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Quote every attribute value and encode the quote character that
  delimits it (and `<`, `>`, `&`) when writing request-derived data into an
  attribute. Never build an attribute value by string concatenation in a
  template, and never place request-derived data in an event-handler or a URL
  attribute without a scheme allow-list.
references: CWE-79
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-XSS_REFLECTED_JS-01
title: Reflected cross-site scripting - unescaped reflection in a script block
script: active/xss.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Do not interpolate request-derived data into JavaScript source.
  Pass it to the page as data - a JSON payload the script reads, or a data-
  attribute - and encode it for that carrier, so `<`, `>` and both quote
  characters can never terminate a string literal or the script element itself.
references: CWE-79
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

# --- DAST-19, modules/dast/active/openredirect.sh (docs/DESIGN.md §7.3) ------
# Appended by the open-redirect ticket. This file is SHARED by every tier-4
# probe and is treated as APPEND-ONLY: a merge conflict in it is resolved by
# keeping BOTH blocks, never by choosing a side.  modules/dast/passive/ no longer
# works this way - §9 now also reserves the `checks-` PREFIX and that directory
# is split one file per owner (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29); splitting is
# optional and per-directory, and this file is deliberately not split under
# peers who are mid-flight.  The
# basename is forced - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path table gives the §9.5
# script-check schema to any file named `checks.rules` at any depth and makes
# every other path E070, so a per-ticket `openredirect-checks.rules` is not a
# legal record file.
#
# Two ids for one defect class because the DAST fingerprint (target, method,
# path_template, param_location, param_name) carries nothing naming the SINK:
# one id would make a `Location` finding and a meta-refresh finding on the same
# parameter collide and dedupe to one. They are also different things to fix.
#
# Tagged `active`, matching the phase table's `active/openredirect.sh:active`
# floor, and NOT `intrusive`: the payloads are URLs, the probe changes no state,
# and it never follows the redirect it detects - so the active-intensity opt-in
# is the gate, and requiring --allow-intrusive on top would be a second,
# undocumented one.

id: DAST-INJ-OPENREDIR_HEADER-01
title: Open redirect via request parameter reflected into the Location header
script: active/openredirect.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-601
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Do not build a redirect destination from request-controlled
  input. Redirect only to a server-side allow-list of paths or destination
  ids, resolving the request value to an entry in that list. Where an absolute
  URL is unavoidable, parse it and compare the resulting HOST against an
  allow-list - never a prefix, substring or "starts with our domain" test,
  each of which is defeated by userinfo, by a target-prefixed subdomain, and
  by scheme-relative and backslash forms.
references: CWE-601
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-INJ-OPENREDIR_META-01
title: Open redirect via request parameter reflected into a meta-refresh
script: active/openredirect.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-601
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Do not build a client-side redirect destination from
  request-controlled input. Emit a meta-refresh only to a server-side
  allow-list of paths, and escape any request-derived value that reaches the
  document so it cannot introduce a new destination. Parsing the host and
  comparing it against an allow-list is the only safe check - a prefix or
  substring test on the URL is defeated by userinfo and by a target-prefixed
  subdomain.
references: CWE-601
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# §7.2 safe-active: HTTP method enumeration (DAST-13, active/methods.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These three share this file with the §7.3 injection records above rather than
# taking a pack of their own, because rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path table
# reserves the basename `checks.rules` repository-wide for the §9.5 schema and a
# `modules/dast/active/methods.rules` would be E070. Peers in this directory
# share the file and resolve the append-only conflict by taking both sides.
#
# The type tag is `safe-active`, matching the phase table's
# `active/methods.sh:safe` floor (modules/dast/engine.sh): lib/checks.sh's
# `checks_intensity_keeps` admits `safe-active` from `--intensity safe` upward,
# so these are reachable one tier below the SQLi records above and one tier
# above the passive checks. Neither carries `intrusive`: the probe sends only
# `OPTIONS` and `TRACE`, both defined as having no effect on the resource (RFC
# 7231 §4.3.7, §4.3.8), and requiring --allow-intrusive on top of the safe-tier
# opt-in would be a second, undocumented gate for a check that mutates nothing.
#
# WHY THE TWO ADVERTISEMENT CHECKS ARE `confidence: medium` AND THE TRACE CHECK
# IS `high`, which is the substance of this ticket rather than a hedge: TRACE is
# MEASURED - the probe sends it, because an echo changes no resource - so the
# finding rests on a response this run received. `PUT`/`DELETE`/`PATCH`/`CONNECT`
# are NEVER SENT, because completing one would create, overwrite or delete a
# resource on a target under audit, so those findings rest on the server's own
# `Allow` header. A claim is weaker evidence than a measurement and the record
# says so, rather than presenting both at one confidence and letting the reader
# guess which is which.
#
# One check id per method CLASS, not per method name. The DAST fingerprint
# carries (target, method, path_template, param_location, param_name), and the
# probe sets `loc_method` to the method being REPORTED - so `PUT` and `DELETE` on
# one path are already two findings under one id, with no collision. Minting
# `DAST-METHOD-PUT-01`, `_DELETE-01` and `_PATCH-01` separately would add three
# ids whose remediation text is identical and whose only distinguishing
# component the fingerprint already carries.

id: DAST-METHOD-TRACE_ENABLED-01
title: HTTP TRACE is enabled (Cross-Site Tracing)
script: active/methods.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Disable TRACE at the web server and at every proxy in front of it
  (`TraceEnable off` in Apache httpd; an explicit rejection in nginx, which does
  not implement TRACE but will pass it upstream), and disable IIS's TRACK in the
  same change, since it is not covered by the TRACE setting. Do not rely on
  HttpOnly to keep a session cookie away from page script while TRACE is
  answered: the echo returns the cookie the browser attached. Verify at the edge
  as well as the origin, because a CDN or load balancer that answers TRACE
  itself reintroduces the issue after the origin is fixed.
references: CWE-693
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
references: RFC 7231 section 4.3.8

id: DAST-METHOD-WRITE_ADVERTISED-01
title: A state-changing HTTP method (PUT, DELETE or PATCH) is advertised as allowed
script: active/methods.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-749
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Confirm the method is intended on this route. If it is not, refuse
  it at the application router and at the web server or proxy in front of it
  rather than relying on a framework default - PUT, DELETE and PATCH are
  commonly left enabled by a permissive WebDAV, upload or REST module nobody
  chose. If the method IS intended, check that it enforces authentication AND
  per-object authorisation on every path that reaches it (an Allow header is a
  routing fact and says nothing about who may act), that it validates the target
  path against traversal, and that a written file can never be served back as
  executable content. This check establishes acceptance from the server's own
  advertisement and never sends the method, so confirm it yourself against a
  resource you are willing to lose, or read the route table.
references: CWE-749
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
references: RFC 7231 section 6.5.5

id: DAST-METHOD-CONNECT_ADVERTISED-01
title: HTTP CONNECT is advertised as allowed, which is an open-proxy shape
script: active/methods.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-441
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Unless this host is deliberately a forward proxy, refuse CONNECT
  outright at the web server and at every proxy in front of it. If it IS a
  forward proxy it must never be reachable from an untrusted network: bind it to
  the interface its clients are on, require authentication, and restrict the
  destination host and port set to an explicit allow list, since an unrestricted
  CONNECT to port 25 is how a host ends up relaying mail. Confirm from
  off-network that the port is not reachable, because a proxy intended for
  internal use that is exposed by a cloud security-group default is the ordinary
  way this happens.
references: CWE-441
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
references: RFC 7231 section 4.3.6

# --- DAST-17, modules/dast/active/pathtraversal.sh (docs/DESIGN.md §7.3) -----
# Appended by the path-traversal ticket, keeping this file's append-only
# convention (see the DAST-19 note above): a merge conflict here is resolved
# by keeping BOTH blocks, never by choosing a side.

id: DAST-INJ-PATH_TRAVERSAL-01
title: Path traversal via request parameter
script: active/pathtraversal.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-22
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Resolve every request-derived filename against a fixed base
  directory using the platform canonical-path check, and reject the request
  when the resolved path escapes that directory, rather than filtering
  "../" substrings. Prefer an indirect reference (an id looked up in a
  server-side allowlist or database) over accepting a path or filename from
  the client at all, and run the process under an account with no read
  access outside the intended content directory.
references: CWE-22
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# §7.3 active injection: OS command injection (DAST-16, active/cmdi.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bounded time-based blind command injection (CWE-78), reusing active/sqli.sh's
# inject_engine.sh and time-based mechanism. Tagged `active`, matching the
# phase table's `active/cmdi.sh:active` floor. The injected sleep duration is
# clamped into 1..10s BEFORE substitution so a probe can never become a DoS -
# the bound is load-bearing and applied, not advisory.

id: DAST-INJ-CMDI_TIME-01
title: OS command injection (time-based blind) via request parameter
script: active/cmdi.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-78
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never pass request-derived data to a shell. Call the target program
  directly with an argument vector so no field is re-parsed by a shell interpreter,
  rather than building a command string. Where a shell is unavoidable, use a strict
  allow-list of permitted values rather than escaping, and run under a
  least-privilege account.
references: CWE-78
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# §7.3 active injection: server-side template injection (DAST-18, active/ssti.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Arithmetic-only template injection (CWE-1336, with CWE-94 as the code-injection
# parent it specialises). Tagged `active`, matching the phase table's
# `active/ssti.sh:active` floor. The probe sends ONE multiplication of two small
# integers wrapped in one engine's delimiters and treats the EVALUATED product -
# never the reflected expression - as the signal; it reads no file, runs no
# command, walks no object graph, and mutates nothing, not even template-local
# state. Payloads are vendored in modules/dast/payloads/ssti-expressions.txt.
#
# FOUR IDS, ONE PER TEMPLATE-ENGINE FAMILY, for the same reason the three SQLi
# techniques above have three: the DAST location profile (target, method,
# path_template, param_location, param_name) carries NO component naming the
# engine, so one id would collide a Jinja2 hit and a FreeMarker hit on the same
# parameter into a single fingerprint and findings_merge would silently keep
# whichever sorted first. rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.1.1 NAME allows `_`, so the
# family lives in NAME under the shared `INJ` family alongside its peers.
#
# Severity `critical` and confidence `high` together: an engine that compiled
# request-derived text is normally a direct path to remote code execution, and
# an evaluated arithmetic result is an unambiguous observation rather than an
# inference from a differential (which is why the boolean/time-based peers above
# carry `medium`).

id: DAST-INJ-SSTI_BRACES-01
title: Server-side template injection via a double-brace expression in a request parameter
script: active/ssti.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1336
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never concatenate request-derived data into a template SOURCE
  string. Render a fixed, developer-authored template and pass the user value
  in as a CONTEXT VARIABLE, so the engine treats it as data rather than as
  syntax to compile. Where user-authored templates are a product requirement,
  render them in the sandbox the engine documents (Jinja2
  SandboxedEnvironment, Twig SandboxExtension, Liquid's strict mode) with a
  deny-by-default allow-list of exposed objects and methods, and treat that
  sandbox as defence in depth rather than a boundary.
references: CWE-1336
references: CWE-94
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-SSTI_DOLLAR-01
title: Server-side template injection via a dollar-brace expression in a request parameter
script: active/ssti.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1336
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never concatenate request-derived data into a template SOURCE
  string or into an expression the container evaluates. Render a fixed,
  developer-authored template and pass the user value in as a CONTEXT
  VARIABLE. On the JVM, set FreeMarker's TemplateClassResolver.SAFE_RESOLVER
  and disable the new-object builtin, restrict Spring EL evaluation to a
  SimpleEvaluationContext rather than a StandardEvaluationContext, and keep
  request data out of any string a view resolver treats as a fragment or
  template name.
references: CWE-1336
references: CWE-94
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-SSTI_ERB-01
title: Server-side template injection via an ERB-style expression in a request parameter
script: active/ssti.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1336
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never render a template whose SOURCE contains request-derived
  data - in Ruby, never pass user input to ERB.new or Erubi, and in Node never
  pass it to ejs.render as the template argument. Render a fixed,
  developer-authored template file and supply the user value through the
  locals/binding hash instead, so the engine treats it as data rather than as
  code to compile.
references: CWE-1336
references: CWE-94
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-SSTI_SMARTY-01
title: Server-side template injection via a single-brace expression in a request parameter
script: active/ssti.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1336
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never build a template string from request-derived data and never
  call Smarty's string resolver on it. Assign the user value with assign() and
  render a fixed template file, so the engine treats it as data rather than as
  syntax to compile. Where user-authored templates are a product requirement,
  enable Smarty's secure mode with an explicit allow-list of permitted
  modifiers, functions and PHP calls, and treat it as defence in depth rather
  than a boundary.
references: CWE-1336
references: CWE-94
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-XXE_ENTITY-01
title: XML parser processes a DOCTYPE-declared entity (candidate XXE sink)
script: active/xxe_ssrf.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-611
owasp: A05:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Disable DOCTYPE processing (or at minimum external general and
  parameter entity resolution) in every XML parser this endpoint can reach.
  For Java, set the JAXP/DocumentBuilderFactory/SAXParserFactory/
  XMLInputFactory FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING and disallow-doctype-decl
  features; for libxml2-based parsers (PHP, Python lxml, Ruby Nokogiri), do
  not enable entity substitution or external entity loading; for .NET, use
  XmlReaderSettings with DtdProcessing.Prohibit. A parser that still
  substitutes an entity declared in the request body confirms these settings
  are not applied, which is the precondition for XML External Entity (XXE)
  attacks including SSRF and local file disclosure.
references: CWE-611
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-INJ-XXE_SSRF-01
title: XML external entity causes the server to fetch an in-scope URL (XXE-driven SSRF)
script: active/xxe_ssrf.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-918
owasp: A10:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Disable external entity resolution in this endpoint's XML
  parser (see DAST-INJ-XXE_ENTITY-01's remediation for the per-platform
  settings) - this is the confirmed-impact case, not merely a candidate.
  Independently, apply an SSRF allow-list to any code path that lets
  request-derived data choose a server-side fetch destination: resolve the
  destination server-side, then require the resolved address to match an
  explicit allow-list of intended hosts before the fetch is issued, rejecting
  private/loopback/link-local ranges and any redirect that leaves the
  allow-listed set.
references: CWE-918
references: CWE-611
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A10_2021-Server-Side_Request_Forgery_%28SSRF%29/

id: DAST-INJ-SSRF_PARAM-01
title: Server-side request forgery via request parameter
script: active/xxe_ssrf.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-918
owasp: A10:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never let request-derived data choose a server-side fetch
  destination directly. Resolve the destination host server-side and require
  the RESOLVED address (not the string a client supplied) to match an
  explicit allow-list of intended hosts before issuing the fetch, reject
  private/loopback/link-local/CGN ranges outright, and re-validate on every
  redirect hop rather than trusting the first check alone.
references: CWE-918
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A10_2021-Server-Side_Request_Forgery_%28SSRF%29/

id: DAST-INJ-NOSQLI_ERROR-01
title: NoSQL injection (error-based) via request parameter
script: active/nosqli.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-943
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never build a NoSQL query, $where expression, or filter object
  directly from request-derived data. Use the driver's parameterised query
  builder so a request value is always bound as a literal, never as an
  operator key or JS fragment, and disable $where/server-side JS evaluation
  entirely unless a specific query genuinely requires it. Return a generic
  error so a driver/ODM message never reaches the client.
references: CWE-943
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-NOSQLI_BOOLEAN-01
title: NoSQL injection (operator/object injection, boolean-based) via request parameter
script: active/nosqli.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-943
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never build a NoSQL query, $where expression, or filter object
  directly from request-derived data. Reject a request parameter outright
  when the field expects a scalar and the received value is an object/array
  (an operator-injection object literal such as {"$ne":null} arriving where
  a string is expected), validate every request value's type server-side
  before it reaches the driver, and disable $where/server-side JS evaluation
  entirely unless a specific query genuinely requires it.
references: CWE-943
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-CRLF_HEADER_INJECTION-01
title: CRLF injection - request parameter reaches raw HTTP response header construction
script: active/crlf.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-113
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never build a response header by concatenating request-derived
  data into raw header text. Use the web framework or HTTP library's own
  header-setting API, which refuses or strips an embedded CR/LF, rather than
  writing a header line by hand. Strip or reject CR and LF from any
  request-derived value before it can reach header construction, and encode
  values placed into a redirect target, a cookie, or any other reflected
  header field.
references: CWE-113
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-CRLF_RESPONSE_SPLITTING-01
title: CRLF injection allows a full forged second HTTP response (response splitting)
script: active/crlf.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-113
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never build a response header by concatenating request-derived
  data into raw header text. Use the web framework or HTTP library's own
  header-setting API, which refuses or strips an embedded CR/LF, rather than
  writing a header line by hand. This is the confirmed-impact case, not
  merely a candidate - request-controlled input can forge a complete second
  HTTP message, which any cache or proxy in front of this target may split
  into and serve as a separate cached response (cache poisoning) or use to
  smuggle a request past a front-end's own inspection. Treat this as an
  urgent fix and audit any cache/CDN in front of the target for the same
  exposure.
references: CWE-113
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-PROTOPOLLUTION_ERROR-01
title: Prototype pollution (error-based) via __proto__/constructor.prototype request parameter
script: active/protopollution.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1321
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never pass request-derived data straight into a recursive merge,
  assign, extend, or deep-clone function. Reject or strip the keys
  __proto__, constructor, and prototype from every level of any object built
  from request data before it reaches a merge, and prefer JSON.parse with a
  reviver that drops those keys over a naive parse-then-merge. Upgrade any
  vulnerable merge library and call Object.freeze(Object.prototype) at
  process startup as a defence in depth.
references: CWE-1321
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-PROTOPOLLUTION_MARKER_REFLECTED-01
title: Prototype pollution confirmed - a __proto__/constructor.prototype-injected property leaked into a later, unrelated response
script: active/protopollution.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1321
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Never pass request-derived data straight into a recursive merge,
  assign, extend, or deep-clone function. This is the confirmed-impact case,
  not merely a candidate - a value planted through one request was read back
  by an unrelated later request, which means Object.prototype (or an
  equivalently shared, long-lived object) is contaminated for every request
  this process serves. Treat this as an urgent fix: reject or strip
  __proto__/constructor/prototype keys from request-derived objects before
  any merge, build request-derived option objects with Object.create(null),
  and restart the affected process after patching to clear any pollution
  already in memory.
references: CWE-1321
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/
